A block's class depends on what its selector references resolve to, not
only on how they are written. Two blocks with the same module, scope,
binding and text whose `$(x)` names two different blocks get two classes,
and `generate` writes both.

(a) `x` is shadowed between two functions that each bind `s` to the same
text.

  $ cat > shadow.ml <<EOF
  > let x = [%css "color: red"]
  > let f () = let s = [%css "&.\$(x) { color: blue; }"] in s
  > let x = [%css "color: green"]
  > let g () = let s = [%css "&.\$(x) { color: blue; }"] in s
  > EOF
  $ ../../standalone.exe --impl shadow.ml -o shadow.out.ml
  $ styled-ppx.generate shadow.out.ml
  /* This file is generated by styled-ppx, do not edit manually */
  .css-1t9sors{color:red;}
  .css-10ybg52.css-1t9sors{color:blue;}
  .css-3thnxa{color:green;}
  .css-1wa9w6r.css-3thnxa{color:blue;}

(b) A structure-level `open` between the two blocks changes what `x` names.

  $ cat > opens.ml <<EOF
  > module A = struct let x = [%css "color: red"] end
  > module B = struct let x = [%css "color: green"] end
  > open A
  > let f () = let s = [%css "&.\$(x) { color: blue; }"] in s
  > open B
  > let g () = let s = [%css "&.\$(x) { color: blue; }"] in s
  > EOF
  $ ../../standalone.exe --impl opens.ml -o opens.out.ml
  $ styled-ppx.generate opens.out.ml
  /* This file is generated by styled-ppx, do not edit manually */
  .css-872j4f{color:red;}
  .css-8d7ucg{color:green;}
  .css-bmhqmb.css-872j4f{color:blue;}
  .css-d73jqb.css-8d7ucg{color:blue;}
